  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 124 PM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A non-tropical low pressure system is expected to \nform near the northern Bahamas by late Friday. Where it tracks this \nweekend is still highly uncertain, which influences overall impacts \nto central NC.\n\nHigh pressure and overall quiet weather will persist across central \nNC through the end of the work week. We continue to track, however, \nthe potential for unsettled weather late Saturday into early next \nweek Monday. \n\nA non-tropical low pressure system is forecast in the majority of \nthe guidance to form near the northern Bahamas by late Friday. The \nNational Hurricane Center has started to issue outlooks for the \ndeveloping system. Their early afternoon update indicated that the \nsystem could become a subtropical or tropical storm while it moves \nnorthward or northeastward near or offshore of the southeastern US \ncoast through the weekend. Right now, the formation of tropical \ncharacteristics is 40-percent through 7 days. \n\nWhile the track of this system is still highly uncertain between \nmodel and ensemble solutions and also run-to-run inconsistencies, \nthere is a broad consensus that impacts, if any, would be centered \non Sunday into early Monday. A look at the latest 12z model and \nmachine-learning solutions show some degree of improvement with \nrespect to the track. The 12z AI-GFS and CMC models take the track \nof the storm near or just along the NC coast Sunday into Sunday \nnight, while the 12z EC-AIFS and operational GFS take the system \nfurther west near the Coastal Plain or just inland of the coast. The \nformer solutions would bring less rainfall and minimal impacts to \ncentral NC, while the latter solutions would bring upwards of 2 \ninches of rain to portions of the area and scattered gusty winds. \nThe median of the 00z LREF ensemble cluster solutions seem to agree \nwith the former solutions, with a track near or just off the NC \ncoast. It is worth pointing out that we may not know full details \nuntil late this week once the system fully forms. Please continue to \nmonitor forecast trends in the coming days.\n\nBeyond this weekend, ensembles are indicating another shot of \nunsettled weather near the Thanksgiving holiday. Given that it is \nstill more than seven days out, confidence is not great. However, \nthere are increasing signals for a more active storm track during \nthis time frame.",
